A key (or other lock opening tool) is a primary candidate for being
#adjusted to the same letter in every game. "k" would be sensible; in my
games it's "e" since that is where the rogue's starting lockpick tends to
be assigned.

> -> "unlock the door (No)?"
> y: "you unlock the door."
> o: "in what direction?"
> -> "the door opens."
>
> That's gotta be one of the LOOOONGEST dialogues in the game, just to open a 
> door. Compare that with typing K(arrow)K(arrow)K(arrow) until it crashes 
> open.
>
> So is there any reason to go through all that? Will you randomly be attacked 
> by a giant demon from hell for kicking doors? 

Several reasons. The obvious one is that kicking a door in destroys it, so
you lose the tactical advantage of a closable door. Also, kicking makes a
noise that can wake sleeping monsters (nymphs for example are generated
sleeping and best left so when you start seeing them).

